# Normalize line endings to LF in both the repo and the working tree.
#
# On Windows (core.autocrlf) git kept rewriting the working copy to CRLF, which made
# every `git add` print a "LF will be replaced by CRLF" warning and produced phantom
# whitespace diffs. Pinning `eol=lf` stops that at the source; it is content-neutral
# (the repo already stores LF) and applies progressively to files as they are touched.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts must keep LF even on a Windows checkout — CRLF breaks a `#!/bin/sh` shebang.
*.sh   text eol=lf
*.bash text eol=lf
*.bat  text eol=crlf

# Never run text conversion over binaries: a CRLF rewrite would corrupt them.
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.gif    binary
*.ico    binary
*.webp   binary
*.pdf    binary
*.woff   binary
*.woff2  binary
*.ttf    binary
*.otf    binary
*.db     binary
*.sqlite binary
*.sqlite3 binary
*.zip    binary
*.gz     binary
*.whl    binary

# Third-party artifacts are pinned by SHA-256, so the repository must store the bytes upstream served.
#
# `* text=auto eol=lf` above silently rewrote `vendor/echarts.min.js` at commit: that file genuinely
# carries 45 CRLF pairs (measured against the CDN bytes, which hash to the recorded pin), so the blob
# in git was a *different file* than the one that was reviewed — and CI, checking out the LF blob,
# failed the pin while this box, reading its own stale working copy, passed it. A pin that a checkout
# can invalidate is not a provenance record. The geo basemap is in the same boat: its test asserts
# provincial content, which is only meaningful about exact bytes.
backend/static/vendor/** -text
backend/static/geo/**    -text
